// THE NAME COLLISION between a namespaced TYPE and a PascalCase IMPL FUNCTION,
// and the reason it needs a fixture of its own.
//
// `type Day.Hours Int` and `impl Day { fn Hours(d: Day): Int }` are BOTH legal
// and both declare the spelling `Day.Hours`. MEASURED on the interpreter:
//
//   - This file, with only the FUNCTION spelling used, PASSES. So
//     `Day.Hours(Day(3))` resolves to the impl function.
//   - A file using BOTH spellings is a FRONT-END ERROR — "argument 1: expected
//     Day, got Int" and "argument 1: expected Day.Hours, got Int" at one
//     position, the checker reporting both candidates. That is why the
//     constructor cases live in dotted_distinct.nomi and this file has none: the
//     two spellings cannot coexist in one program, so they cannot be one
//     fixture.
//
// WHAT IT DISCRIMINATES. native.go asks implCall BEFORE the namespaced-
// constructor arm. Without this file that ordering is UNOBSERVABLE: moving the
// arm above implCall survived every guard in the package, measured, because
// every other dotted callee in the corpus and in the fixtures ends in a
// snake_case member name that can never be a type. A PascalCase impl function
// is the one shape where the two namespaces really do collide, and with the arm
// above implCall this program is claimed as a constructor and refused
// `argument type mismatch | Day.Hours wraps Int, got Day` — a refusal on a
// program the interpreter runs.
//
// So the surviving mutant was a MISSING TEST rather than a free arm, which is
// the reading differential_test.go's header lists second of five.
type Day Int

type Day.Hours Int

fn day_value(d: Day): Int {
    Day(n) = d
    n
}

fn hours_value(h: Day.Hours): Int {
    Day.Hours(n) = h
    n
}

impl Day {
    fn Hours(d: Day): Int {
        day_value(d)
    }
}

test "a PascalCase impl function wins over a same-named namespaced type" {
    assert Day.Hours(Day(3)) == 3
    assert Day.Hours(Day(41)) == 41
}

test "the namespaced type is still usable as a TYPE under the collision" {
    // The collision is about the CALL spelling only. `hours_value` above is
    // annotated `Day.Hours` and destructures it, so the TYPE side and the pattern
    // side are both lowered in this file even though no constructor call can
    // appear in it. Its result is unobservable here by construction, so this case
    // asserts only that the file containing it runs — the type-side coverage is
    // the declaration, not the assertion.
    assert day_value(Day(5)) == 5
}
